---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Integrating omics measured on different individuals" toc={false}>
<style>
  .hero { padding: 4.5rem 0 1rem; max-width: 46rem; }
  .hero h1 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .hero .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .fig { margin: 3rem 0 1rem; }
  .fig svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .cap { color: var(--fg-3); font-size: .84rem; max-width: 44rem; margin: .6rem 0 0; }
  .band { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); margin-top: 4rem; padding-top: 3rem; }
  .band > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .prose { max-width: 44rem; }
  .dot { animation: pulse 4s ease-in-out infinite; }
  @keyframes pulse { 0%,100% { opacity: .55 } 50% { opacity: 1 } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .dot { animation: none; opacity: .85 } }
  .flow { stroke-dasharray: 4 5; animation: dash 5s linear infinite; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  @keyframes dash { to { stroke-dashoffset: -45 } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .flow { animation: none } }
  /* var() does not resolve inside SVG presentation attributes, so fills and
     strokes that follow the theme are set as CSS properties instead. */
  .fig svg text { font-family: var(--sans); }
  .fig .t-strong { fill: var(--fg); font-weight: 600; }
  .fig .t-soft   { fill: var(--fg-2); }
  .fig .t-mute   { fill: var(--fg-3); }
  .fig .t-mono   { fill: var(--fg-3); font-family: var(--mono); }
  .fig .dot      { fill: var(--accent); }
  .fig .panel    { fill: var(--bg-2); stroke: var(--rule); }
  .fig .panel-hi { fill: var(--bg-2); stroke: var(--accent); }
  .fig .stem     { stroke: var(--rule); stroke-width: 1.5; }
</style>

<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">R package · disjoint-cohort multi-omics</p>
  <h1>Omics measured on different individuals, integrated without inventing the link.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Tissue is consumed by the assay, so the transcriptome, the proteome and the lipidome of a
    disease model are usually measured in different animals. CHORALE finds the biological
    programmes several of those cohorts carry together, states how far the data constrain the
    relationship between them, and reports the checks the result survived.
  </p>
  <div class="btnrow">
    <a class="btn" href={base + '/tutorial'}>Run the tutorial</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href={base + '/why'}>Why it exists</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/lescailab/chorale">Source</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="fig reveal">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 900 286" role="img" aria-label="Three cohorts of animals with no individual in common, each yielding a latent axis, compared through the experimental design and through curated biology to give one shared programme.">
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="g" x1="0" x2="1">
        <stop offset="0" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.14"/>
        <stop offset=".5" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.4"/>
        <stop offset="1" style="stop-color:var(--accent);stop-opacity:.14"/>
      </linearGradient>
    </defs>

    {[0,1,2].map((i) => {
      const x = 90 + i * 310;
      const label = ['transcriptome','proteome','lipidome'][i];
      const n = ['221 animals','72 animals','96 animals'][i];
      return (
        <g>
          <text x={x + 85} y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" class="t-strong">{label}</text>
          <text x={x + 85} y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="t-mono">{n}</text>
          {[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7].map((j) => (
            <circle class="dot" cx={x + 12 + (j % 4) * 48} cy={58 + Math.floor(j / 4) * 18}
                    r="5" style={`animation-delay:${(i * 8 + j) * 0.18}s`} />
          ))}
          <rect x={x} y="100" width="170" height="56" rx="8" class="panel"/>
          <text x={x + 85} y="124" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" class="t-soft">latent axis</text>
          <text x={x + 85} y="142" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11.5" class="t-mute">recovered separately</text>
          <line class="stem" x1={x + 85} y1="156" x2={x + 85} y2="190"/>
        </g>
      );
    })}

    <line class="flow" x1="175" y1="190" x2="725" y2="190"/>
    <rect x="235" y="198" width="430" height="34" rx="8" class="panel-hi"/>
    <text x="450" y="220" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" class="t-strong">compared on the design, and again on curated biology</text>
    <line class="stem" x1="450" y1="232" x2="450" y2="250"/>

    <rect x="0" y="250" width="900" height="36" rx="9" fill="url(#g)"/>
    <text x="450" y="273" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5" class="t-strong">one shared programme, carried by all three</text>
  </svg>
  <p class="cap">
    No animal appears twice. The cohorts are joined by what they have in common, which is the
    experimental design and a shared vocabulary of biological processes, never by a constructed
    pairing between individuals.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>What it gives you that a pairwise workflow does not</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">01</span>
      <h3>One assignment, not many</h3>
      <p>Correspondence is solved once over all modalities together, so a programme cannot be
         assembled by chaining pairwise decisions through a single mistaken link.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">02</span>
      <h3>Two channels that can disagree</h3>
      <p>Factors are fitted without ever seeing curated pathways, so agreement on biology
         corroborates the result rather than restating it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">03</span>
      <h3>A range, not a fabricated number</h3>
      <p>Where the coupling within an individual is not identified, the output is the set of
         correlations the data cannot exclude.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">04</span>
      <h3>A lipidome in the gene vocabulary</h3>
      <p>A shipped table places lipid classes in the same pathway sets the genes occupy, so
         enzymes and their substrates land together.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">05</span>
      <h3>Selection-aware calibration</h3>
      <p>The null reruns the whole procedure, assignment included, so the p-value accounts for the
         freedom the method had.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="num">06</span>
      <h3>Failure is an output</h3>
      <p>A run in which nothing survives its null says so, and writes empty programme tables rather
         than promoting an unsupported assignment.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>What a result looks like</h2>
  <p>
    One row per modality carrying a programme, with the evidence that supports it and the
    contribution each layer made. This is the first table the report writes.
  </p>

<div class="tablewrap">

| programme | modalities | joint_p | pathway_p | evidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| P3 | RNA, PROT, METAB | 0.005 | 0.008 | design and pathway |
| P1 | RNA, PROT | 0.015 | 0.42 | design only |

</div>

  <p>
    <strong>P3</strong> is carried by all three cohorts. They respond to the disease the same way,
    and they implicate the same biology, and the second was established without the factors ever
    seeing the pathway annotation. <strong>P1</strong> is real but rests on one line of evidence, so
    it ranks below.
  </p>
  <p>
    Alongside it, <code>chorale_leave_one_out()</code> reports what happens to each programme when a
    modality is removed, which is what separates a result integration produced from one a single
    layer produced and the others accompanied.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>Built for the data that exists</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Deposited cohorts</h3>
      <p>Public transcriptomes, proteomes and lipidomes for one disease model, generated by
         different groups in different animals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Uncorrected matrices</h3>
      <p>Batch correction removes the between-modality structure identification uses, so study and
         batch enter as covariates instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tens to hundreds of animals</h3>
      <p>Statistics estimable at the cohort sizes bulk multi-omics resources actually reach, with
         precision that grows with animals rather than with design strata.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>The whole workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Three matrices, three design tables, one call. Everything after it reads results rather than
    configuring the method.
  </p>
<pre><code>containers &lt;- Map(chorale_load, matrices, designs)

fit &lt;- chorale_fit(containers,
                  n_factors     = c(RNA = 4, PROT = 3, METAB = 3),
                  gene_sets     = chorale_genesets(species = "Mus musculus"),
                  feature_space = c(METAB = "lipid"))

chorale_programmes(fit)        # what several cohorts carry together
chorale_leave_one_out(fit)     # whether it needed all of them
chorale_bound(fit)             # what the data cannot determine
chorale_report(fit, chorale_bound(fit), chorale_null(fit, containers), "results/")</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <a href={base + '/tutorial'}>tutorial</a> runs this end to end on simulated data and explains
    every number it prints.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="band prose reveal">
  <h2>Install</h2>
<pre><code># install.packages("devtools")
devtools::install_github("lescailab/chorale")</code></pre>
  <p>
    R 4.4 or later. Bioconductor dependencies resolve through <code>BiocManager</code>.
    The <a href={base + '/tutorial'}>tutorial</a> runs end to end on simulated data, so nothing
    needs downloading to see the whole workflow.
  </p>
</section>
</Base>
